{
  "concept": "For a projectile launched and landing at the same height, range depends on launch speed squared and on sin(2θ) — so range grows as v₀², peaks at 45°, and is identical for complementary angles even though their trajectory shapes differ.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "Panel (a) label and Panel (b) label beneath each plot",
    "Horizontal ground line (y = 0) spanning each panel, serving as both launch and landing level",
    "Small axis key at upper right of panel (a): short vertical arrow labeled y, short horizontal arrow labeled x",
    "Panel (a): three parabolic trajectories from a common origin, all at 45° — orange (v₀ = 30 m/s), purple (v₀ = 40 m/s), teal (v₀ = 50 m/s)",
    "Panel (a): three color-matched velocity arrows at the origin labeled 30 m/s, 40 m/s, 50 m/s, all along a 45° ray",
    "Panel (a): angle arc at origin labeled 45°",
    "Panel (a): dashed vertical drop lines from each trajectory apex to the ground line",
    "Panel (a): three horizontal dimension bars with end ticks below the ground line, labeled R = 91.8 m, R = 163 m, R = 255 m",
    "Panel (b): three parabolic trajectories from a common origin at fixed speed — orange (15°), purple (45°), teal (75°)",
    "Panel (b): label v₀ = 50 m/s beside the steepest launch arrow",
    "Panel (b): three color-matched velocity arrows at the origin with nested angle arcs labeled 15°, 45°, 75°",
    "Panel (b): dashed vertical lines at the shared landing point of the 15° and 75° curves and at the apex of the 75° curve",
    "Panel (b): two dimension bars labeled R = 128 m (shared by 15° and 75°) and R = 255 m (45° curve)",
    "Live readout annotations for the learner-controlled curve: current v₀, θ, apex height, and computed range",
    "Ghost/faded copies of the three original textbook curves in each panel for comparison"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "speedSlider",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "At a fixed 45° launch, range stretches as the square of initial speed — doubling v₀ quadruples R — while the trajectory keeps exactly the same parabolic shape, just scaled.",
      "label": "Panel (a): initial speed v₀ (θ fixed at 45°)",
      "range": [10, 60, 1],
      "default": 30
    },
    {
      "id": "angleSlider",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "At a fixed speed, range follows sin(2θ): it climbs to a maximum at 45° and falls off symmetrically, so a steep lob and a flat drive can cover the same ground.",
      "label": "Panel (b): launch angle θ (v₀ fixed at 50 m/s)",
      "range": [5, 85, 1],
      "default": 45
    },
    {
      "id": "showComplement",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "The complementary angle 90° − θ lands at the identical spot but with a very different apex height and flight time — same range, different path.",
      "label": "Show complementary angle (90° − θ)",
      "default": false
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Baseline: 30 m/s at 45°",
      "narration": "A projectile leaves the origin at 45° with 30 m/s and returns to ground 91.8 m away. The dashed line marks its apex; the bar below the ground line measures its range.",
      "state": { "speedSlider": 30, "angleSlider": 45, "showComplement": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Raise the speed to 40 m/s",
      "narration": "Push v₀ up by a third and the range jumps to about 163 m — much more than a third further. The parabola keeps its shape but scales up in both directions.",
      "state": { "speedSlider": 40, "angleSlider": 45, "showComplement": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Speed squared, not speed",
      "narration": "At 50 m/s the range reaches 255 m. Going from 30 to 50 m/s (×1.67) multiplied the range by 2.78 — exactly 1.67², confirming R ∝ v₀².",
      "state": { "speedSlider": 50, "angleSlider": 45, "showComplement": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Now hold speed, turn the angle down",
      "narration": "With v₀ locked at 50 m/s, drop the angle to 15°. The trajectory flattens and the range shrinks to 128 m — plenty of horizontal speed, but far too little airtime.",
      "state": { "speedSlider": 50, "angleSlider": 15, "showComplement": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Complementary angles share a range",
      "narration": "Turn on the complement: 75° traces a tall, slow arc yet lands on the same 128 m mark as the shallow 15° shot. Same range, wildly different height and flight time.",
      "state": { "speedSlider": 50, "angleSlider": 15, "showComplement": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "45° is the peak",
      "narration": "Sweep back to 45° and the complement coincides with the curve itself — the two solutions merge. This is the single angle that balances airtime against horizontal speed, giving the maximum 255 m range.",
      "state": { "speedSlider": 50, "angleSlider": 45, "showComplement": true }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Range R = v₀² sin(2θ)/g packs both lessons: speed enters squared so it dominates, while the angle enters through sin(2θ), which peaks at 45° and takes the same value for θ and 90° − θ — identical ranges from completely different-looking trajectories.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two stacked panels sharing a consistent horizontal scale out to ~270 m, each with a thin dark ground line and the panel letter (a)/(b) centered below. Panel (a): common origin at the left, three parabolas in orange, purple, teal (increasing size in that order), all launched along a single 45° ray with short color-matched arrows labeled 30, 40, 50 m/s stacked at the origin and a small arc labeled 45°. Light gray dashed verticals drop from each apex. Below the ground line, three left-aligned double-headed dimension bars with end serifs, labeled R = 91.8 m, R = 163 m, R = 255 m, stacked so the shortest is on top. A small axis key sits in the upper right: an up arrow labeled y and a right arrow labeled x. Panel (b): same origin, three parabolas at 15° (orange, low and flat), 45° (purple, widest), 75° (teal, tall and narrow), with nested angle arcs and labeled arrows at the origin and 'v₀ = 50 m/s' beside the steepest arrow. Dashed verticals mark the shared 15°/75° landing point and the 75° apex; two dimension bars below read R = 128 m and R = 255 m. Interactive curve in each panel is drawn bold in its panel color with the textbook curves faded to light gray ghosts; live readouts of v₀, θ, apex height, and R float near the active apex. The complementary curve, when shown, is drawn dashed in the same hue with its own apex marker so the height difference is obvious while both endpoints coincide."
}

